    "title": "Ideation, Prototyping, and Strategic Experimentation",
    "category": "Maker Week, ideation, prototyping",
    "videoUrl": "https://youtu.be/McTh-xzxfRE?si=POvF9YrPcm6HpC64",
    "content": "The fifth week of our Information Systems Honours programme plunged us directly into the messy, unpredictable reality of Maker Week, forcing us to confront the practicalities of ideation and prototyping. Up until this point, our theoretical discussions about strategy and digital innovation felt somewhat controlled, but the requirement to actually design a strategically grounded solution for the informal economy quickly tested our assumptions. The most profound lesson I learned this week was that in the realm of strategic experimentation, failure is not a setback; it is a critical diagnostic tool. This became glaringly obvious during our initial project phase when we attempted to focus our digital solution on local spaza shops. When we confidently pitched our first empathy map to the class and the lecturer, it completely fell apart. The map was disjointed and pointed in too many different directions, leaving the lecturer lost, the class confused, and our group without a clear vision of the exact problem we were trying to solve. Our fieldwork and surveys had revealed a harsh truth: the spaza shop owners did not have a specific, pressing digital problem that they actually wanted us to solve. Realising that we were forcing a solution onto a community that did not need it was a difficult pill to swallow, but it demonstrated exactly why failing fast is an essential strategic capability. 

[cite_start]Embracing this failure allowed us to exercise an agile mindset, completely pivoting our focus to an environment where the pain points were visible and actionable[cite: 292]. We redirected our energy towards the hair and beauty sector, specifically targeting students on campus who run their own local salons and barbershops. This pivot changed everything. Because we were dealing with a demographic that actively struggled with specific issues like last-minute cancellations, disorganised bookings, and a lack of clear financial tracking, we were finally able to gather rich, meaningful information. [cite_start]It taught me that social innovation cannot be forced; it requires genuine participation and involvement from stakeholders who actually feel the friction of the problem we are trying to alleviate[cite: 288]. By shifting our target audience, we moved away from inventing problems to actually addressing the lived realities of informal entrepreneurs. 

However, finding the right problem was only the beginning; the ideation phase presented its own unique set of frustrations. We were instructed to engage in divergent thinking by coming up with twenty distinct ideas each. Initially, this exercise sparked a tremendous amount of creativity, pushing us to think far outside the box to generate novel concepts for the beauty and wellness space. [cite_start]We explored everything from a "Wig-Life" maintenance ledger to community co-ops[cite: 326, 329]. Yet, as we pushed past the tenth or twelfth idea, a distinct sense of repetition set in. The core problems faced by these campus barbers are fundamentally quite simple, meaning that our elaborate solutions soon started sounding exactly the same, just dressed in different technological buzzwords. This repetition was incredibly frustrating, but it led to a vital strategic realisation. In constrained, informal environments, innovation does not necessarily mean inventing twenty wildly different technological paradigms. Often, the most effective digital strategy is a single, simple methodology executed flawlessly. We learned that while divergent ideation is necessary to explore the boundaries of a problem, the ultimate goal is to converge on a solution that is practical rather than overly complex.

This lesson in practicality heavily influenced our decision-making when it came time to select our final prototypes. As someone who appreciates the cutting edge of technology, the solution I was most reluctant to let go of was the augmented reality integration. [cite_start]We had conceptualised a "Virtual Try-On" feature where clients could use their phone cameras to instantly visualise how different hairstyles, fades, or wigs would look on them before booking[cite: 337]. [cite_start]Conceptually, it is a brilliant idea that aligns with the rapid technological innovation of a modern digital society[cite: 295]. However, strategic experimentation requires us to brutally assess economic viability and business capability. Building a functional augmented reality tool is highly resource-intensive; it is too expensive, technically demanding, and would take far too much time for an informal campus business to realistically implement or afford. Letting go of the Virtual Try-On feature was a humbling exercise in strategic discipline. It reinforced that as an IS professional, I cannot let my fascination with high-tech solutions override the actual feasibility and sustainability of the project within its specific context. 

Moving into the prototyping phase, we are applying these lessons of simplicity and speed directly to our execution strategy. Instead of sinking weeks into developing a complex, hard-coded application from scratch, we plan to utilise quick AI app builders or construct wireframes using dummy data at the backend. Time constraints are a reality, and our primary objective is not to present a finished software product, but to quickly get a working concept into the hands of the barbers. [cite_start]By running these rapid, low-fidelity test runs, we can explain the core concepts—such as slot swapping and automated loyalty rewards—and observe how the users interact with the system[cite: 336, 338]. This approach perfectly encapsulates the essence of prototyping in design thinking: we are building just enough to test our assumptions, allowing the community's feedback to guide the next iteration before any significant resources are committed.

Reflecting on Maker Week as a whole, my understanding of what makes a successful Information Systems strategy has deeply matured. I now view uncertainty, failure, iteration, and learning not as project risks to be avoided, but as the very capabilities that drive genuine innovation. The discomfort of having our first idea dismantled in front of the class, the frustration of repetitive brainstorming, and the disappointment of dropping a beloved high-tech feature were all necessary steps in finding a true strategic fit. I am walking away from this week with a profound respect for the iterative process, completely convinced that the best digital solutions for South Africa’s informal economy will not come from getting it right the first time, but from having the humility to fail fast, learn immediately, and design alongside the people who will actually use the system."
